WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.994
A private model does not automatically make private use safe.

2
00:00:03.994 --> 00:00:11.828
Users, service accounts, applications, agents, administrators, and support tools all need appropriate permissions.

3
00:00:11.828 --> 00:00:22.274
A user may be allowed to ask general questions but not access sensitive document collections, call production tools, export logs, or trigger automated actions.

4
00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:25.730
Access control should cover more than the front door.

5
00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:35.561
Teams need rules for model access, retrieval collection access, tool permissions, administrative functions, service identities, and audit trails.

6
00:00:35.561 --> 00:00:52.689
Role-based access can be useful, but AI systems often benefit from capability-style permissions as well: this user can summarize approved documents, that service can call a specific retrieval index, and this workflow requires approval before an action is taken.

7
00:00:52.689 --> 00:01:03.672
Policy layers can decide which model is allowed, which data class may be used, which tools are enabled, which outputs require review, and when human approval is required.

8
00:01:03.672 --> 00:01:09.740
These decisions should be close enough to the workflow that they are testable and reviewable.

9
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:14.963
Sensitive actions should fail closed when policy context is missing or unclear.

10
00:01:14.963 --> 00:01:22.720
Agents and tool-using systems need especially clear boundaries because they can perform work beyond text generation.

11
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.327
They may read, write, search, call APIs, open tickets, or trigger business processes.

12
00:01:28.327 --> 00:01:37.236
Least privilege, administrative separation, approval gates, and structured audit records help keep those capabilities accountable.

13
00:01:37.236 --> 00:01:45.685
Sovereign AI depends on visible policy enforcement, not informal trust that every user and tool will behave within expectations.
